<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02009</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.2: The Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b7f7d3c0-39c6-400a-ab8d-8338efcf3183</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Here are two sets of equations for quadratic functions you saw earlier. In each set, the expressions that define the output are equivalent. In other words, all the equations in Set 1 define the same function. The same is true for Set 2: all the equations define the same function but they are just written in different forms.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
       <emphasis>Set 1:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00003">\(f(x)=x^2+4x\)</para>
      <para id="para-00004">\(g(x)=x(x+4)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00005">\(h(x)=(x+2)^2−4\)</para>
      <para id="para-00006">
       <emphasis>Set 2:</emphasis>
    </para>
      <para id="para-00007">\(p(x)=-x^2+6x−5\)</para>
      <para id="para-00008">\(q(x)=(5−x)(x−1)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00009">\(r(x)=-1(x−3)^2+4\)</para>
      <para id="para-00010">The expression that defines \(h\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">vertex form</term>. We can show that it is equivalent to the expression defining \(f\) by expanding and simplifying the expression:</para>
      <para id="para-00011">\(\begin{array}{rcl}(x+2)^2-4&amp;=&amp;(x+2)(x+2)-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+2x+2x+4-4\\&amp;=&amp;x^2+4x,\;\mathrm{which}\;\mathrm{is}\;f(x)\end{array}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00012">If this simplified expression is factored, then we arrive at the expression that defines function \(g\): \(x^2+4x=x(x+4)\). The expression that defines \(g\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">factored form</term>. The simplified expression that defines function \(f\) is written in <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">standard form</term>.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00013">Show that the expressions defining \(r\) and \(p\) are equivalent.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>s</emphasis><emphasis>olution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your Answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">Expanding and simplifying function \(r\), \(-1(x−3)^2+4 gives -1(x^2−6x+9)+4\), which equals \(-x^2+6x−9+4\) or \(-x^2+6x−5\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para id="para-00017">For questions 2 – 3, examine the graphs representing the quadratic functions.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="ffff5156-2bf7-4567-96b2-7aa7f7191d18" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">

            <para id="para-00018">Examine the graph of the function \(h\).</para>
            <para id="para-00019">What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(h\)?</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! -2</para>
            <para id="para-00021">The correct answer is -2.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_467b3ca3-8550-4222-ba3f-a1c76865ea1b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            
            <para id="para-00022">What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(h\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00023">Correct! -4</para>
            <para id="para-00024">The correct answer is -4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0773894f-eec9-475a-82ba-23df40c73c4f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">

            <para id="para-00025">Examine the graph of the function \(r\).</para>
            <para id="para-00026">What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(r\)?</para>
            
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00027">Correct! 3</para>
            <para id="para-00028">The correct answer is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="abdf1679-d62b-4b66-bdb8-979b6c361039" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            
            <para id="para-00029">What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph of \(r\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00030">Correct! 4</para>
            <para id="para-00031">The correct answer is 4.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00032">Why do you think expressions such as those defining \(h\) and \(r\) are said to be written in vertex form?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00034">The numbers in vertex form seem to give the coordinates of the vertex of the graph. When a positive number is added to the \(x\) in the parentheses, the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be negative—which is the opposite of that number. When a number is subtracted from \(x\), the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex seems to be positive—which is the opposite of what it looks like.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <note id="note-00001">
        <title>Why Should I Care?</title>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_17_03_01.png"/>
          </media>
        
        <para id="para-00035">When you launch an object, it does not travel in a straight line. Instead, it follows a curved path called a parabola. This means you have to account for distance, height, and angle if you want to hit your target. You can use quadratic equations to find these measurements.</para>
        <para id="para-00036">The next time you throw a ball, think about the parabolic path that it follows.</para>
      </note>
    </section>

    <note class="self-check" id="note-00002">
      <para id="para-00037">Which equation is equivalent to \(y=(x-3)^2-9\)?</para>
    </note>

    <section id="sect-00003"><title>Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
      <para id="para-00038">Quadratics can be written in different equivalent forms:</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Standard form: \(y=ax^2+bx+c\)</item>
        <item>Factored form: \(y = (x + a)(x + b)\)</item>
        <item>Vertex form: \(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00039">Example</para>
      <para id="para-00040">Write \(y=(x+2)^2-9\) in standard and factored forms.</para>
      <para id="para-00041">Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
      <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
        \(y=(x+2)(x+2)-9\)</para>
      <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
        \(y=x^2+2x+2x+4-9\)</para>
      <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
        \(y=x^2+4x-5\)</para>
      <para id="para-00045">Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
      <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - From the standard form, find the factors of the trinomial expression \(y=x^2+4x-5\).</para>
      <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Examine the trinomial expression.
        \(x^2+4x-5\)</para>
      <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial.
        \((x + 5)(x - 1)\)</para>
      <para id="para-00049">The factored form is \(y = (x + 5)(x - 1)\).</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00003">
      <title>Try It: Equivalent Forms of Quadratics</title>
      
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          
          <para id="para-00050">Find the equation in standard form that is equivalent to \(y=(x-2)^2+4\).</para>
        
        <para id="para-00051">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        
        </problem><solution id="sol-00007">
        <para id="para-00052">Here is how to find the equivalent standard form:</para>
        
        <para id="para-00053"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Expand the vertex form to multiply the binomials.
          \(y=(x-2)(x-2)+4\)</para>
        
        <para id="para-00054"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Multiply the binomials.
          \(y=x^2-2x-2x+4 + 4\)</para>
        
        <para id="para-00055"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Combine like terms.
          \(y=x^2-4x+8\)</para>
      
      </solution></exercise></note>
      </section>
  </content></document>